  "ci-cd-tools": [
    {
      question: "What's the difference between CI and CD?",
      answer: "CI (Continuous Integration) means every code change automatically runs tests and builds — catching bugs early before they merge. CD (Continuous Delivery) extends this by automatically preparing releases for deployment, while Continuous Deployment goes further and pushes every change that passes tests directly to production. Most teams start with CI, then add CD for automated releases, and only pursue Continuous Deployment when they have robust testing and monitoring in place."
    },
    {
      question: "GitHub Actions vs Jenkins — which should I use?",
      answer: "GitHub Actions is simpler to set up, integrates tightly with GitHub repositories, and offers generous free minutes for open source. Jenkins is more flexible, runs on your own infrastructure, and has a massive plugin ecosystem for custom workflows. For most teams, GitHub Actions wins on simplicity and maintenance. Use Jenkins when you need full control over runner hardware, have expensive CI workloads, or work in regulated industries requiring self-hosted infrastructure."
    },
    {
      question: "How do I reduce CI build times?",
      answer: "The biggest wins are caching (dependencies, build artifacts, compiled output), parallelization (splitting tests across multiple runners), and better test selection (only running affected tests per change). Use layer caching in Docker builds, cache node_modules and similar dependency directories, and configure pytest, Jest, and other test frameworks to run tests in parallel. For large projects, sharding tests across multiple runners with tools like Test Distribution can cut build times from 20 minutes to under 5."
    },
    {
      question: "What is a good CI/CD pipeline structure?",
      answer: "A solid pipeline has: (1) code quality checks (linting, formatting), (2) unit and integration tests, (3) build verification, (4) security scanning (dependency vulnerabilities, secrets detection), (5) deployment to staging, (6) integration tests in staging, (7) optional manual approval gate, (8) production deployment. Keep the pipeline fast — every minute of CI time is a developer waiting. Use caching aggressively, skip unnecessary steps for docs-only changes, and invest in flaky test detection and quarantine."
    },
    {
      question: "How do I handle secrets in CI/CD pipelines?",
      answer: "Never store secrets as plain text in configuration files or environment variables that appear in logs. Use your CI/CD platform's secret management (GitHub Actions secrets, GitLab CI variables, Vault by HashiCorp) to inject secrets at runtime. For Kubernetes deployments, use native secret management or external secrets operators. Rotate secrets regularly, use short-lived credentials where possible, and audit access logs for who retrieved which secrets and when."
    }
  ],
  "api-testing-tools": [
    {
      question: "What is the difference between API testing and unit testing?",
      answer: "Unit tests verify individual functions and classes in isolation — they test logic. API testing validates the contract between services — that endpoints return correct status codes, headers, and response bodies for given inputs. API tests are broader in scope and catch integration issues unit tests miss: wrong data types, missing fields, authentication failures, rate limiting, and how the API behaves with malformed requests. Unit tests run faster but API tests give more confidence that your services work together correctly."
    },
    {
      question: "Postman vs Insomnia — which API tool is better?",
      answer: "Postman is more feature-rich with extensive collaboration features, mock servers, and a larger community with shared collections — better for teams managing many APIs. Insomnia is leaner, faster, and has a cleaner interface — better for individual developers or small teams who value simplicity. Both handle REST, GraphQL, and gRPC well. Bruno is an emerging open-source alternative that stores collections as version-control-friendly markdown files rather than a proprietary format. Choose Postman for team collaboration and Insomnia for a lighter experience."
    },
    {
      question: "How do I automate API tests in CI/CD?",
      answer: "Export your Postman or Insomnia collections as a JSON file, then run them via the CLI runner (newman for Postman, insomnia-test for Insomnia) in your pipeline. Chain requests using environment variables and test scripts to validate responses and pass data between calls. For code-based approaches, write tests in Jest or pytest using the HTTP client of your choice. Store test credentials as secrets in your CI platform, use separate environments (staging vs production), and set aggressive timeouts to catch slow endpoints."
    },
    {
      question: "What is contract testing in API development?",
      answer: "Contract testing verifies that an API provider and its consumers agree on the request and response format — without deploying an integration environment. Provider-driven contract testing (Pact) has consumers define what they expect, then the provider runs those expectations against their actual responses. Consumer-driven contracts have the provider define their schema and consumers validate against it. Contract testing catches breaking changes before they reach consumers, and is essential for microservices architectures where many teams depend on shared APIs."
    },
    {
      question: "How do I test GraphQL APIs?",
      answer: "GraphQL testing tools include Postman (which has native GraphQL support), Insomnia, and specialized tools like GraphQL Voyager for schema exploration. Write queries for each operation, assert on the response shape and specific field values, and test error cases by sending invalid variables. For contract testing, use libraries like dredd-graphql-interface that validate your schema against actual responses. Test subscriptions with mock WebSocket servers, and validate query complexity and depth limits to prevent malicious queries."
    }
  ],
